def ghost_head(parent, latest, stakes, root):
    children = {}
    for child, par in parent.items():
        children.setdefault(par, []).append(child)
    weight = {}
    for v, block in latest.items():          # add each vote's stake to the block and all its ancestors
        b = block
        while b is not None:
            weight[b] = weight.get(b, 0) + stakes[v]
            b = parent.get(b)
    head = root
    while head in children:
        head = max(children[head], key=lambda c: (weight.get(c, 0), c))
    return head

exercises.solution:
```python
def ghost_head(parent, latest, stakes, root):
    children = {}
    for child, par in parent.items():
        children.setdefault(par, []).append(child)
    weight = {}
    for v, block in latest.items():      # each vote supports its block and every ancestor
        b = block
        while b is not None:
            weight[b] = weight.get(b, 0) + stakes[v]
            b = parent.get(b)
    head = root
    while head in children:
        head = max(children[head], key=lambda c: (weight.get(c, 0), c))
    return head
```

The last test shows the difference from a longest-chain rule: a branch of six blocks with one vote loses to a single block with two. GHOST counts support for whole subtrees, so votes for different blocks on the same branch reinforce each other rather than splitting. Counting only each validator's *latest* message means an equivocating validator cannot be counted twice. Production clients compute this incrementally with cached weights, since the block tree and votes change every slot, and they apply further rules (such as a "proposer boost" for timely blocks) that this exercise leaves out.
